The Model Context Protocol just shipped its biggest revision since it
launched, and it's not an incremental one. The 2026-07-28 spec (finalized
after a release candidate) drops protocol-level sessions entirely — SEP-2575
and SEP-2567 both remove the initialization handshake and the
`Mcp-Session-Id` header from the Streamable HTTP transport. If you're running
MCP servers in production the way we are for our agent framework, this is a
genuine migration, not a changelog to skim.

## The wire format actually changed

Under the old (2025-11-25) protocol, everything after `initialize` rides on a
session ID:

```
POST /mcp HTTP/1.1
Mcp-Session-Id: 1868a90c-3a3f-4f5b
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call",
 "params":{"name":"search","arguments":{"q":"otters"}}}
```

That header is what pinned you to one server instance — whichever instance
handled `initialize` is the only one that knows this session exists. Under
2026-07-28, there's no session at all. Every request is self-describing:

```
POST /mcp HTTP/1.1
MCP-Protocol-Version: 2026-07-28
Mcp-Method: tools/call
Mcp-Name: search
Content-Type: application/json

{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/call",
 "params":{"name":"search","arguments":{"q":"otters"},
           "_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo":
                    {"name":"my-app","version":"1.0"}}}}
```

Protocol version, client identity, and client capabilities now travel inside
`_meta` on every call instead of being negotiated once. `Mcp-Method` and
`Mcp-Name` move into headers too, so a gateway can route and authorize
without parsing the JSON body at all.

## A minimal simulation of why that matters

The claim everyone makes is "now you can round-robin without sticky
sessions." Rather than take that on faith, here's a small Python simulation —
two worker instances, a naive round-robin balancer, nothing shared between
them, which is exactly the failure mode session pinning was built to avoid.

```python
import itertools
import random

class Worker:
    """A single MCP server instance behind a plain round-robin balancer.
    Nothing is shared between workers -- session state, if any, only
    ever lives in the instance that created it."""

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name
        self.sessions = {}  # session_id -> context, stateful-era only

    # --- Old protocol (2025-11-25): session-pinned ---
    def initialize(self):
        session_id = f"sess-{random.randint(1000, 9999)}"
        self.sessions[session_id] = {"turns": 0}
        return session_id

    def handle_stateful(self, session_id, tool, args):
        if session_id not in self.sessions:
            raise LookupError(
                f"{self.name}: no record of {session_id} -- "
                f"this instance never saw the initialize call"
            )
        self.sessions[session_id]["turns"] += 1
        turn = self.sessions[session_id]["turns"]
        return f"{self.name} handled {tool}({args}) -- turn {turn}"

    # --- New protocol (2026-07-28): stateless ---
    def handle_stateless(self, request):
        params = request["params"]
        meta = params["_meta"]["io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo"]
        return (
            f"{self.name} handled {params['name']}({params['arguments']}) "
            f"for client {meta['name']} -- no session lookup needed"
        )


def round_robin_pool(workers):
    return itertools.cycle(workers)


def demo_stateful_breaks_under_round_robin():
    print("=== Old protocol: Mcp-Session-Id, sticky sessions required ===")
    workers = [Worker("worker-a"), Worker("worker-b")]
    pool = round_robin_pool(workers)

    init_worker = next(pool)
    session_id = init_worker.initialize()
    print(f"initialize() landed on {init_worker.name}, got {session_id}")

    for i in range(3):
        worker = next(pool)
        try:
            result = worker.handle_stateful(session_id, "search", {"q": "otters"})
            print(f"  call {i + 1}: {result}")
        except LookupError as e:
            print(f"  call {i + 1}: FAILED -- {e}")


def demo_stateless_just_works():
    print("\n=== New protocol (2026-07-28): self-contained requests ===")
    workers = [Worker("worker-a"), Worker("worker-b")]
    pool = round_robin_pool(workers)

    request = {
        "jsonrpc": "2.0",
        "id": 1,
        "method": "tools/call",
        "params": {
            "name": "search",
            "arguments": {"q": "otters"},
            "_meta": {
                "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientInfo": {"name": "my-app", "version": "1.0"}
            },
        },
    }

    for i in range(3):
        worker = next(pool)
        result = worker.handle_stateless(request)
        print(f"  call {i + 1}: {result}")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    random.seed(7)
    demo_stateful_breaks_under_round_robin()
    demo_stateless_just_works()
```

Running it is the whole argument:

```
=== Old protocol: Mcp-Session-Id, sticky sessions required ===
initialize() landed on worker-a, got sess-6305
  call 1: FAILED -- worker-b: no record of sess-6305 -- this instance never saw the initialize call
  call 2: worker-a handled search({'q': 'otters'}) -- turn 1
  call 3: FAILED -- worker-b: no record of sess-6305 -- this instance never saw the initialize call

=== New protocol (2026-07-28): self-contained requests ===
  call 1: worker-a handled search({'q': 'otters'}) for client my-app -- no session lookup needed
  call 2: worker-b handled search({'q': 'otters'}) for client my-app -- no session lookup needed
  call 3: worker-a handled search({'q': 'otters'}) for client my-app -- no session lookup needed
```

Two out of three calls fail under naive round-robin with session pinning —
not because anything is broken, but because a round-robin balancer has no
way to know a session only exists on one instance. That's the entire reason
MCP deployments needed sticky routing or a shared session store in the first
place. Strip the session out of the protocol, and the failure mode is gone
by construction, not by careful ops work.

## What else that buys you

- **Multi round-trip requests.** Servers can now ask for missing parameters
  or confirmations mid-operation without holding a long-lived bidirectional
  stream open.
- **Cacheable list results.** Tool and resource listings carry TTL and
  cache-scope hints instead of forcing a fresh call every time.
- **Authorization hardening.** RFC 9207 issuer validation, a move toward
  Client ID Metadata Documents, and credentials bound to a specific
  authorization server.

## The part that'll actually cost you time

Tasks — the mechanism for long-running operations — got pulled out of core
and into a formal extension, alongside MCP Apps and Enterprise Managed
Authorization. If anything in your stack depended on Tasks being part of the
base protocol, that's a real code change, not a version bump.

There's a twelve-month deprecation window for what's removed, and old and new
aren't guaranteed to talk to each other. All four Tier-1 SDKs — TypeScript,
Python, Go, C# — already support it, with Rust in beta.

## Where we're landing

Our agent framework runs its own MCP servers behind Kubernetes, and the
session-affinity dance was never fun to operate. Losing it is a
straightforward win, and the simulation above is close to literally what
changes for us: fewer ways for a request to land somewhere it can't be
served. The Tasks extraction is the part we're actually budgeting time for —
anything built assuming Tasks was baked into core needs a real look before we
touch the new spec in production.
